<TopicPage slug="subjects/business/intermediate/people-and-organisations">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Organisations are <strong>systems of people coordinated toward outcomes</strong> — and most
    business failures attributed to "strategy" or "market" are coordination, incentive or hiring
    failures. The craft: pick people well, align incentives with truth, motivate with more than
    money, and let the culture be the residue of real decisions.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="hiring, incentives, motivation, culture — the four levers" widget="equationStep" config={peopleCfg} />

  <h2>Organisational design principles</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Conway's law applies to companies:</strong> communication structures get copied into products. Design the teams for the architecture you want (the "inverse Conway manoeuvre").</li>
    <li><strong>Two-pizza teams and coordination cost:</strong> every added person adds communication paths (n(n−1)/2) — small teams with clear missions outperform large committees. Brooks's law, again.</li>
    <li><strong>Psychological safety is the team superpower:</strong> Google's Project Aristotle found it the top predictor of team performance — the freedom to ask, admit and disagree without punishment.</li>
    <li><strong>Remote work redesigns, not relocates:</strong> distributed work shifts value from presence to written clarity and async process — the organisations that thrived redesigned rituals, not just locations.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Brooks, Mythical Man-Month; Pink, Drive; Edmondson, The Fearless Organization; Duhigg, "What Google Learned From Its Quest" (NYT, 2016).</p>
</TopicPage>
